<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cacicazgo of Higüey</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1504 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Taíno chiefdom of eastern Hispaniola, the last on the island to be conquered, ending with the execution of Cotubanamá in 1504.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Higüey occupied the eastern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, now the eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. It was one of the five principal chiefdoms when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> arrived in 1492, and its people had close contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mona-passage">Mona Passage</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Higüey was ruled by paramount caciques, Cayacoa and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotubanama">Cotubanamá</a>, from villages on the coastal plain. Its people were reputed fierce warriors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. They farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mona-passage">Mona Passage</a>. Caves such as those of the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotubanama-national-park">Cotubanamá National Park</a> hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> rock art.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Uprisings against Spanish abuses in 1503-04 were crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-esquivel">Juan de Esquivel</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotubanama">Cotubanamá</a> was captured and hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, ending the last independent chiefdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. Its lands passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo">Captaincy General of Santo Domingo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1200 CE – 1504 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 1